A pretrained model’s default representation or adaptation method is not necessarily the best fit for every task. I first encountered this in speech research at IIIT Hyderabad, where acoustic features and intermediate encoder representations affected what a model could reconstruct and detect. At Samsung Research, related questions arose while personalizing language models: how much of a model needed to be adapted, and how could it learn under the constraints of a mobile device? These experiences shaped my interest in speech and language models whose representations, learning methods, and execution are designed around the task. At MBZUAI, I hope to deepen my understanding of NLP and investigate how language and speech models can be adapted effectively under limited computational resources.

In IIIT Hyderabad’s Speech Processing Lab under Prof. Anil Kumar Vuppala, I worked on whispered-to-normal speech conversion. Whispered speech lacks the vocal-fold excitation present in normal speech, so a model must infer missing information from the remaining acoustic cues. I explored richer spectral representations in a HuBERT-based pipeline, improving reconstruction under whispered and noisy conditions. Their higher dimensionality increased computational cost, which I controlled through spectral smoothing and principal-component selection.

I continued studying speech representations in my undergraduate Honours research on distinguishing typical disfluencies from atypical stuttering. Comparing layers of self-supervised speech encoders, I found that HuBERT’s fifth layer, out of 24, achieved 97% F1 across disfluency categories and outperformed its final-layer representation. I contributed to the IIITH-TISA stuttered-speech corpus and co-authored the resulting paper, published at Interspeech 2025. Together, these projects made me interested in how speech models capture task-relevant information—from acoustic features to representations at different depths—and how that understanding can guide model adaptation.

Alongside research, I led six undergraduates in developing Waveform-Wizard, an open-source Python platform that replaced our lab’s proprietary MATLAB speech-analysis workflows and was presented at ICASSP 2025 Show & Tell. I later joined Samsung Research, where I worked on an on-device inference runtime supporting CPU, GPU, and NPU execution. Integrating runtime updates, resolving deployment issues, and developing an automated GPU caching tool gave me a practical understanding of how model and systems choices affect deployment.

After returning to Samsung Research full-time, I worked with Samsung Research Korea on NNTrainer through a project on privacy-preserving on-device LLM personalization. Conventional fine-tuning of a 1 GB model ran out of memory on the target device. I combined 4-bit base weights with LoRA adapters trained using quantization-aware training, and introduced weight checkpointing and selective activation recomputation. These changes reduced peak memory to under 0.5 GB. Once training fit in memory, I designed an asynchronous CPU–NPU dispatch mechanism to address the execution bottleneck, making training eight times faster than CPU-only execution.

The work has since extended to larger models: in our evaluations, we trained Qwen3-4B with multi-batch training within an 8 GB memory budget, achieving over 96% accuracy on SST-2. We are preparing a paper on the work for ACL 2027. This experience made me interested in how parameter-efficient adaptation and model compression affect not only resource use, but also a language model’s ability to learn and retain task knowledge.

I then investigated whether adaptation necessarily required backpropagation. GGML’s Llama runtime offered a faster precompiled forward path on the NPU but lacked backward support. I explored zeroth-order optimization, which estimates updates using forward evaluations alone. Because full-weight perturbations exceeded device limits, I restricted updates to a gradient-informed low-rank subspace and used orthogonal projections to retain knowledge across tasks. On a mobile NPU, the method matched or exceeded standard LoRA in accuracy and retention and completed 1,000 steps in 20 minutes—five times faster than prior on-device zeroth-order training. This work is under review at MLSys 2027.

I am now investigating a hybrid optimizer in which a device performs local zeroth-order updates while a server periodically provides first-order guidance. The question I want to study further is how the update space and frequency of guidance affect convergence and retention across tasks. A stronger foundation in NLP and optimization would help me evaluate these methods beyond the particular models and hardware on which I have worked.

My interest has also extended from adaptation to generation. Working with Dr. Prateek Kesarwani, I adapted speculative decoding for a device–server handoff: the device drafts candidate tokens while a more capable server verifies them in parallel. Because the device generates the draft itself and retains its execution state, returning control to it can avoid re-prefilling a server-generated continuation. Against full-context server recomputation, this reduced end-to-end latency by 1.4 times and server computation by 2.8 times. I am exploring intermediate-layer exits and parallel drafting, with the goal of understanding how generation methods can use models with different capabilities while preserving output quality.

More recently, I worked on vision-language model reinforcement-learning rollouts using Qwen2.5-VL and vLLM. Optimizing multimodal preprocessing and rollout-side CPU execution reduced rollout-worker time by 20–30% on tested image sets while preserving generated token streams in paired comparisons. I have also joined a collaborative effort on active video understanding, investigating how a model might select additional temporal or spatial evidence under visual-token and memory constraints. This extends my interest in task-relevant representations to multimodal models that must also decide what information to acquire.

MBZUAI’s research-based NLP program would let me bring these interests together through coursework and a master’s thesis. Natural Language Processing and Advanced Natural Language Processing would help me build a broader foundation in language tasks and neural modeling, while Speech Processing would deepen the knowledge I developed through HuBERT. I am particularly interested in Prof. Alham Fikri Aji’s work on efficient NLP and model adaptation, which connects to my interest in retaining task performance under resource constraints. Prof. Hanan Aldarmaki’s research on speech and language representations and transfer learning offers a connection to my speech work and an opportunity to study how representations can support tasks with limited data.

For my thesis, I hope to investigate how representations and update methods can be selected for efficient language or speech model adaptation, and how those choices affect task performance and retention. After graduation, I want to apply what I learn to the development of capable language and speech systems that can be adapted and used in resource-constrained settings.
